  "about": "Rendered PDF page 253, folio 196 (running head \"196 Acoustic Absorbers and Diffusers\"); the table fills the page and closes with a rule after \"Sandstone\", so it does not continue on PDF page 254 (folio 197), which was rendered to check and holds only prose. Title as printed: \"Table 6.5 Typical porosity values for some materials\". Two columns, headings in italic: \"Material\" and \"Typical porosities\"; no unit (porosity is dimensionless, values carried over with factor 1). Rows sit under four bold section headings, \"Fibrous materials\", \"Cellular materials\", \"Granular materials\" and \"Other\", kept here as the group field; inside each group thin horizontal rules band the rows in threes or fours, a typographic aid only. No footnotes. Material names carry superscript reference numbers into the chapter 6 reference list (PDF pages 276-281, printed pp. 219-224); \"Expanded perlite\" carries none. Section 6.4.2 Open porosity introduces it on PDF page 254 (printed p. 197): \"Although the porosity is a key parameter, for many common porous absorbents, the value does not vary greatly and is close to unity. Table 6.5 gives some typical values. Porosity values for the ground can be found in Table 6.7.\" The same section (PDF 251, printed p. 194) states that \"most mineral wools have a porosity of about 0.98\"; that closed pores are to be excluded from the pore volume because they are inaccessible to sound, which is what makes this an OPEN porosity, is said further on, on PDF page 254 (printed p. 197). Ranges printed with an en dash go to ranges; two cells carry an approximately-equals sign (Vermiculite (granular) \"≈0.65–0.68\", Marble \"≈0.005\") and are flagged approximate; Aerogel prints \">0.75\", which the schema cannot hold natively and is stored as a range bounded below (see its note); Glass beads (1.6 and 0.7 mm) prints \"0.34, 0.38\" with no explicit pairing and is kept as reported. Values are printed to two decimals except 0.995, 0.005, 0.4 and 0.1, kept as printed.",
